Happy Thursday,
 
TLDR: ➤ No New Trades

 

Bulls are trying hard to reverse the selling that characterized the final hour on Thursday.
 
We are headed into the end of the month, and the S&P began today down 2.4%.
 
Bulls have a long road ahead because the catalyst for this month's weakness remains unresolved.
 
The war with Iran continues.
 
These reports are never about politics because that discussion is unproductive.
 
However, bond traders keep telling President Trump that he must get energy prices under control.
 
He is not listening.
 
It seems he is trying to make the case that his wars pay for themselves.
 
Fixed-income traders want the war over.
 
Expediency will likely require some form of reparations for Iran.
 
The tension in financial markets right now is all about when the President will make that concession.
 
The longer he waits, the worse it could become for markets because bond traders will continue pushing interest rates higher.
 
Meanwhile, bears are getting squeezed this morning.
 
The MSFT results last night reinforced that AI spending is moving full steam ahead, especially for compute.
 
IREN, APLD, and HUT are all up more than 20% as the AI bubble doom narrative begins to unravel.
 
These stocks are still in downtrends, so we should not get too excited.
 
However, the important names—MU, STX, and WDC—are all on the verge of reversing their downtrends.
 
We have MU headed to at least $915.
 
STX is more problematic for bulls.
 
If the early gains hold, shares should make a new high at $1,110.
 
WDC has key resistance at $555.
 
A gap above that level sets up a rally toward $750.
 
We like the way AMZN is finding a bid ahead of earnings. We need a close above $240, though.
 
DELL is in the same boat. The chart looks terrific, assuming the early gains hold.
 
STZ remains the weakest stock in the consumer staples group. Shares should decline to $125.
 
We still plan to sell half at that level. The order remains open.
 
MDB is still having trouble getting through $335. One more day for this holding.
 
We added C on Wednesday.
 
We are comfortable maintaining a short position in the banking group.
 
No new trades today.

 

