Happy Wednesday,
 
TLDR: ➤ Buy C September 18th $130 puts at $6.40 or less, day order only

 

The S&P 500 is skidding again after a strong recovery on Tuesday as President Trump ramps up the war rhetoric.
 
West Texas Intermediate crude oil is up 6.5% to $84.39 per barrel. Brent crude is trading at $89.76 per barrel, up 6.8%.
 
Traders can see the plotline for an end-of-the-month rally, but President Trump keeps stepping on the narrative.
 
He is playing with fire.
 
If energy prices remain elevated, traders could force a credit crisis.
 
They will stop funding U.S. Treasuries and demand higher interest rates.
 
That narrative is beginning to creep into the discussion.
 
Fitch, the debt ratings agency that famously missed the 2009 financial crisis, said today that the AI bubble could cause a credit crisis.
 
We can debate whether that is true.
 
It does not matter.
 
The discussion about credit matters.
 
When institutional desks begin worrying about credit, they take off risk. Full stop. Rates rise.
 
President Trump cannot win a war with bond traders. They control the money and, ultimately, the agenda.
 
We are adding a new put position in C.
 
We see shares headed to at least $120, and possibly $117.15 to fill the April gap.
 
Yesterday, STZ closed poorly, as expected.
 
MDB and DELL closed stronger.
 
For those positions, we need closes this week above $336 and $411, respectively.
 
We saw the bullish setups in software stocks like WDAY, SAP, ADBE, and NOW, but we did not want to own calls on stocks locked in steep downtrends.
 
Most of those names have now reached their first resistance levels.
 
The Federal Open Market Committee policy statement is due today at 2:00 p.m.
 
These events always create volatility, but we remain more concerned that President Trump could step on every attempt to rally stocks.
 
New Order
➤ Buy C September 18th $130 puts at $6.40 or less, day order only

 

