Happy Friday,
 
TLDR: No New Trades

 

The S&P 500 is modestly lower. That is a problem.
 
Bulls are getting no bounce following the sharp decline over the past two sessions, even as oil prices ease.
 
Sure, it is Friday after a particularly poor week.
 
Traders do not want to take a lot of long exposure home over the weekend. That is understandable.
 
What is more worrisome is the sharp rise in 10-year Treasury yields and the collapse in high-yield corporate bonds.
 
This is how credit crises begin.
 
Until yesterday, fixed-income traders were willing to buy junk bonds despite higher energy prices and inflation. They assumed the war with Iran was over.
 
It is now clear they no longer believe that.
 
We wrote back in May that President Trump cannot win a war with bond traders.
 
They can simply refuse to buy U.S. debt and demand higher yields.
 
Their message is straightforward. End the war. Get oil prices under control.
 
He does not appear to be listening, so the bond market is tightening financial conditions to get his attention.
 
That is the process. That is how the game is played.
 
The good news is the QQQ is nearing an important technical level.
 
There is a gap at $676, the May 5 high. Buyers could return there looking for a tradable rally.
 
There are also a few charts that have our attention.
 
AMGN looks constructive, although there is resistance at $376.
 
GE is breaking out, but that move is tied to defense spending. We have an upside target of $380.
 
We continue to like MO as a hedge against market volatility.
 
AMZN is not acting great, but shares are holding key support. We are looking for a bounce into next week's earnings report.
 
DELL's relative strength has been nothing short of remarkable.
 
If the QQQ finds support near $676, DELL has the potential to emerge as one of the market leaders alongside NVDA.
 
We also wrote down two short ideas.
 
ALAB appears poised to trend back toward $265.
 
AAOI looks like a cinch to trade back to $88.50.
 
Unfortunately, there is not enough open interest in the September options for our purposes, so we are passing.
 
Nothing new today.
 
The market is approaching an important inflection point. If buyers defend support, we expect plenty of opportunities next week.
 
Stay patient and keep your powder dry.

 

