Happy Thursday,
 
TLDR: No New Trades

 

The news today is all good, yet stocks are mixed to lower.
 
Some of this is expected. Bulls are still having trouble getting through the S&P 500 downtrend line near 7,525.
 
It is a little disconcerting that bears are stepping into a weaker-than-expected payroll report.
 
The economy produced only 57,000 jobs in June, well below expectations of 110,000. That should be bullish for stocks because it reduces the need for the Federal Reserve to raise interest rates.
 
Bears should be covering short positions.
 
They are not. At least not yet.
 
TSLA sold 480,126 vehicles in the second quarter, versus expectations of 406,000.
 
That is a blowout report.
 
Shares are down 6% as traders sell the news. This is hard to explain.
 
Business is booming. Shares should be much higher.
 
We will hold until Monday. Perhaps there will be some late buying today.
 
MO is rebounding after successfully testing its 20-day moving average.
 
MDB is modestly lower. However, the stock continues to look strong in a much better software environment.
 
Somehow, we missed HOOD on Wednesday.
 
We thought our order was on the market, which is strange. Next time we will simply use a market order.
 
We wrote down AAPL for today.
 
The stock is headed toward $317 as traders bet the Trump administration will allow the company to use banned Chinese memory chips.
 
More broadly, this is another example of traders looking to buy laggards.
 
Nothing new today.
 

 

