Happy Wednesday,
 
TLDR: ➤ Buy TOL August 21st $170 Calls at $5.10 or Less

 

The S&P 500 is bouncing modestly this morning, but bulls need to do much better.
 
President Trump continues to support financial markets with comments about abundant oil supplies flowing through the Strait of Hormuz.
 
The message is working.
 
West Texas crude has fallen below $70 per barrel. Under normal circumstances, stocks should be responding much more aggressively to that development.
 
We wrote yesterday that the S&P could finish near 7,340, its rising 50-day moving average. The index ultimately closed at 7,365 after successfully testing that support level intraday.
 
Our expectation was that bulls would come out this morning and immediately take a run at resistance near 7,436.
 
So far, the effort has been underwhelming.
 
The problem, in our view, is that some of the market's biggest winners have not corrected enough to allow capital to rotate elsewhere.
 
We continue to believe leadership needs to broaden.
 
Semiconductor and AI infrastructure stocks have carried the market for months. At some point, names like INTC and MU need to digest gains while software stocks recover from deeply oversold conditions.
 
That rotation is not happening yet.
 
In fact, some of the same leadership groups continue to attract buyers. In fiber optics, GLW is pushing to new highs. That is not the type of action you typically see when money is rotating aggressively away from technology leadership.
 
To be clear, we are not bearish.
 
Interest rates are falling. Energy prices are falling. Financial conditions are becoming more accommodative by the day.
 
As a result, housing and hospitality stocks are beginning to attract attention.
 
TOL, LEN, and AAL all look interesting here. We have upside targets of $168.40, $101.20, and $19.90, respectively.
 
On the bearish side, LMT appears vulnerable to a move back toward $480. The challenge is that the option chain is relatively thin, making risk management more difficult than we would prefer.
 
MDB closed reasonably well on Tuesday but is once again getting dragged lower by weakness across the software group.
 
The stock is dramatically oversold and still appears capable of rallying back toward $332. We will reassess the position if and when shares reach that level.
 
TSLA remains sluggish.
 
The company needs to respond more aggressively to concerns surrounding the Texas accident story. Right now, that headline is weighing on sentiment and preventing the stock from gaining traction.
 
New Orders
 
➤ Buy TOL August 21st $170 Calls at $5.10 or Less Day order only
Do not chase this trade. Patience matters, especially in a market that continues to reward disciplined entries.

 

