Happy Tuesday,
 
TLDR: No New Trades

 

Stocks opened sharply lower this morning as traders reacted to interest-rate forecasts and reports that China now has the world's fastest supercomputer.
 
In our view, neither development changes the bigger picture.
 
Professional money managers are suddenly worried that the Federal Reserve could raise interest rates as soon as July.
 
We think that is highly unlikely.
 
The Fed typically avoids major policy surprises in the months leading up to a significant election cycle, and there is little evidence that policymakers are eager to tighten financial conditions right now.
 
Meanwhile, headlines about China's new LineShine supercomputer are generating plenty of attention.
 
The machine is officially the fastest supercomputer in the world.
 
The problem is that raw computing speed is not the same thing as AI leadership. LineShine is enormous, consumes staggering amounts of power, and appears to rely on brute-force computing rather than the efficient AI-focused architectures that are driving innovation elsewhere.
 
Bears are trying to turn this into another DeepSeek moment.
Investors will remember that DeepSeek headlines temporarily erased trillions of dollars in AI-related market value. What many forget is that DeepSeek ultimately became a buying opportunity.
 
Markets learn. Traders adapt. The AI investment story survived just fine.
 
Still, technology stocks are getting hit hard this morning.
What matters is that the S&P 500 opened near its rising 50-day moving average around 7,340 and immediately found buyers. That level is important.
 
Today could set the tone for the remainder of the month.
 
Bulls need to defend this area and prove that institutional money is still willing to step into weakness. So far, that is exactly what is happening.
 
ENPH and SNOW were both on our radar heading into the session.
 
Only SNOW is showing any real strength, and even that move is fairly modest.
 
We are content to wait. There is no reason to force a trade when conviction is lacking.
 
TSLA is under pressure after finishing strongly on Monday.
 
Last night, a Tesla Model 3 was involved in a tragic accident in Texas that resulted in the death of a 74-year-old woman.
 
According to reports, the driver claimed Tesla's driver-assistance software was engaged at the time. It is important to remember that Tesla's systems are designed to be driver-supervised.
 
For now, traders are selling first and asking questions later. We suspect the stock will stabilize once the company has an opportunity to investigate and provide additional details.
 
MDB closed well on Monday but opened weaker once again this morning.
 
That is not ideal, but we continue to believe a short-covering rally back toward $332 is likely. We will reassess the situation once shares reach that level.
 
The market feels trapped between two outcomes. Either the S&P 500 closes near today's highs and confirms that buyers remain in control, or it drifts back toward the 7,340 support area by the closing bell.
 
We cannot find any easy trades on either side of the tape right now. Believe us, we are looking.
 
Today feels choppy.
 
Stand aside for one more session.

 

