Happy Monday,
 
TLDR: No New Trades

 

The S&P 500 opened higher after several news outlets reported that U.S. and Iranian officials have agreed on a roadmap to end the conflict in Iran.
 
Traders are selling into the news, which is exactly what traders tend to do. There is nothing surprising about that.
 
What is a bit surprising is the continued weakness in some of the larger semiconductor names. AVGO, NVDA, and several others appeared to be repairing their charts. Instead, they are under pressure again this morning.
 
We do not believe this is a sign that something is fundamentally wrong with big technology.
 
This is a momentum market.
 
Traders are buying what is working and selling what is not. Capital continues to flow toward leadership while laggards struggle to attract meaningful bids. That is the defining characteristic of this tape.
 
The names that are working remain BE, ALAB, and CRDO.
 
The challenge is that we are reluctant to chase them. To get attractive option pricing, traders are often forced well out of the money. If momentum stalls, even briefly, those positions can become difficult to manage.
 
There is no reason to force trades simply because stocks are moving.
 
We are also monitoring a pair of new bullish setups.
 
ENPH needs a close above $55 to become interesting. SNOW is shaping up as well, but we would prefer to see shares trade convincingly above $243 before becoming aggressive.
 
TSLA is acting better, even as SPCX pulls back.
 
We continue to believe TSLA is setting up for a move higher. The stock needs a close above $418, but we remain confident that level will be reclaimed. Remember, this is primarily an end-of-quarter vehicle delivery story. Bears should be very careful heading into those numbers.
 
MDB reversed nicely on Friday but opened weaker again this morning.
 
We are willing to give bulls an opportunity to regroup. The broader issue remains the same. Software stocks simply are not attracting sustained capital flows. MDB is getting caught in that crosscurrent for now.
 
We also missed the NOC put trade.
 
In truth, we were not even close to getting filled. Shares closed near their lows on Friday and opened lower again this morning. The stock is now approaching our downside target. Sometimes the best trade is the one that got away. This one appears to be missed.
 
We need to get going.
 
That said, forcing trades is rarely the answer. The market is rewarding patience and punishing urgency. The right setups will come.
 
Nothing new today.

 

