Happy Thursday,
 
TLDR: ➤ Buy NOC August 21st $490 Puts at $6.00

 

Stocks are higher after President Trump moved up the signing date for a deal to end the conflict with Iran.
 
It is important to understand what is happening here.
 
Two weeks ago, fixed-income traders pushed interest rates higher because they wanted the President to end the war.
 
He responded because it is impossible to fight bond traders. They have all the leverage.
 
Now President Trump is trying to keep financial markets buoyant heading into the summer and the midterm elections.
 
He believes stronger stock prices are a vote of confidence in his policies. As a result, investors should expect him to step into periods of market weakness with favorable headlines whenever possible.
 
The problem is that traders like to sell good news.
 
This is not political. It is simply how markets work. Traders handicap events, establish positions ahead of those events, and then sell into the news once it arrives. We are seeing some of that dynamic today.
 
We remain extremely bullish, but leadership needs to broaden.
 
For months, AI infrastructure and industrial equipment have carried the market. That leadership has been impressive, but bulls need strength to expand into other groups if this rally is going to have real staying power.
 
As an aside, CAT remains one of the strongest industrial stocks in the market. Ironically, it is also an AI infrastructure story. Through its Solar Turbines subsidiary, the company has become an important supplier of power infrastructure for next-generation data centers, including xAI's Colossus project.
 
We expected software stocks to take the baton from semiconductors while the chip names digested gains.
 
That has not happened.
 
Semiconductors continue to run hard. ARM, MU, LRCX, and many others remain firmly in uptrends. Software stocks, on the other hand, have yet to fully participate.
 
MDB is a disappointment.
 
Shares opened below support at $332. That is not what bulls wanted to see. The good news is that the trading day is still young. Let's see if buyers can reclaim that level and repair the technical damage before the close.
 
TSLA needs to get back above $400 this week. Resistance is beginning to form near $403. We are willing to give the stock another day or two, but bulls need to regain momentum soon.
 
We sold MRNA at our second target on Wednesday. Shares traded as high as $67.70 this morning. We will gladly look for a second entry if the stock provides a favorable pullback.
 
We are adding one new put position today.
 
Defense stocks are under pressure as the Iran conflict moves toward a conclusion. Large, traditional contractors are being re-rated as investors focus on a future battlefield increasingly dominated by drones, automation, and lower-cost systems.
 
We believe NOC is vulnerable to that shift in narrative and could decline toward $492.
 
