Happy Wednesday,
 
TLDR: ➤ Sell 1/2 MRNA and pocketed a 111.24% Gain!

 

The action in the S&P 500 is sloppy this morning.
 
That should not come as a surprise with the Federal Open Market Committee policy statement just hours away.
 
Traders remain focused on what new Federal Reserve Chairman Warsh might say about interest rates and inflation. We have a pretty good idea of the message because he has been remarkably consistent. To repeat, he is likely to tolerate an economy running a bit hotter than normal as artificial intelligence works its way through the system and creates longer-term deflationary pressures.
 
What we do not know is how markets will react.
 
Bears will undoubtedly argue that this approach risks allowing inflation to become entrenched. The problem with that argument is that the market is not behaving as if inflation is a major concern.
 
Banks and brokers continue to trade at new highs. Consumer-facing stocks are starting to strengthen again. Take a look at W. The message from price action is clear: traders are not particularly worried about higher rates or runaway inflation.
 
The same leadership names continue to work.
 
You know the list by now: AMAT, KLAC, and LRCX. We also continue to like BE and APLD, but there is little reason to get aggressive ahead of today's policy announcement.
 
One name that has our attention is WDAY.
 
We love the way the stock looks and acts here. The chart appears to be building a major reversal pattern, but patience is warranted. Bulls need to clear $133 before the setup becomes actionable. If that happens, the stock could attract meaningful institutional buying.
 
We sold part of our MRNA position on Tuesday for a mighty 111.24% gain!
 
The stock continues to behave well, and we still believe shares can reach $60. We are adding a new target for the remaining position.
 
MDB tested support near $332 early this morning and immediately attracted buyers. That is exactly what bulls wanted to see. The stock remains on track.
 
TSLA continues to drift as traders wait for quarter-end delivery numbers. The story has not changed. Bulls need a decisive move above $417 to kickstart the next leg higher. Until then, patience is required.
 
Once again, we are content to remain on the sidelines.
 
The market is waiting for the Fed. There is no reason to force trades ahead of a potentially market-moving event. We expect to be much more active on Thursday once investors have had time to digest the policy statement and, more importantly, the market's reaction to it.
 
