Happy Tuesday,
 
TLDR: ➤ No New Trades

 

The S&P 500 is hovering near the flatline as traders take some profits ahead of tomorrow’s Federal Open Market Committee meeting.
 
The concern is that the new Federal Reserve Chairman could say something that unsettles investors.
 
We think we already know the message.
 
He has been remarkably consistent. His view is that artificial intelligence will ultimately prove to be a powerful deflationary force. That framework allows for an economy that runs hotter than traditional models might suggest. Whether bond traders are willing to embrace that view is another question entirely.
 
We are about to find out.
 
From a structural perspective, the market continues to do exactly what it needs to do. Semiconductor stocks are digesting their outsized gains while software names begin to pick up more of the leadership burden.
 
That rotation is healthy.
 
The financial media remains obsessed with SPCX. The company has now surpassed the market capitalizations of MSFT and AMZN and appears to be marching toward GOOGL. The narrative has shifted almost overnight.
 
The same commentators who told investors to avoid SPCX are now arguing that the company is disrupting the hyperscale data center operators.
 
This is mostly noise.
 
While we would never bet against Elon Musk over the long term, the hyperscalers are doing just fine. Demand for compute continues to expand, and there is plenty of room for multiple winners in the AI ecosystem.
 
We added MDB on Monday.
 
Shares sprinted to a high of $364 this morning before giving back some of those gains. That action is perfectly acceptable, although we would like to see the stock finish near the upper end of today's trading range. Support remains near $332.
 
TSLA continues to wrestle with resistance around $417. We are not particularly concerned that shares have failed to follow SPCX higher. The market is treating these stories as linked, but we see them differently.
 
TSLA remains a robotaxi and vehicle delivery story. We continue to believe that story is stronger than most investors appreciate. A decisive move above $417 could change sentiment quickly.
 
MRNA continues to grind higher as management changes begin to take shape. There is very little meaningful resistance until roughly $60, and we continue to expect shares to stretch toward that level.
 
We are also building a watch list of software names that appear poised for larger moves. NET, CRWD, DDOG, and IBM are all beginning to set up constructively. We are not ready to act today, but these are names worth watching closely as the week progresses.
 
The neo-cloud trade is also becoming increasingly interesting. CRWV and APLD both look ready to move higher. BE belongs in that conversation as well, although we believe there is no urgency. A little more patience may provide a better opportunity.
 
For today, we are standing pat.
 
Nothing new.

 

