Happy Monday,
 
TLDR: ➤ Buy MDB August 21st $500 Calls at $7.50 or Less

 

Unsurprisingly, President Trump declared victory in the conflict with Iran over the weekend.
 
Markets do not appear interested in debating the facts on the ground. What matters for investors is that energy prices are falling, bond yields are moving lower, and risk appetite is returning.
 
In many ways, the market is behaving as if everything is back to normal.
 
The decline in oil prices is providing immediate relief to inflation expectations, and bond traders are responding accordingly. Yields are falling, financial conditions are easing, and bears are being forced to cover short positions.
 
Whether the United States ultimately gained anything tangible from the conflict is no longer the market's primary concern. Professional investors are already looking ahead. Capital is beginning to move back into equities, even though June has historically been a challenging month for stocks.
 
We closed HOOD and XEL on Friday afternoon.
 
HOOD is particularly frustrating because shares are sharply higher today. That happens. Trading is a game of probabilities, not certainties. We will look for another entry point in the future. For now, there is significant resistance near $102.70.
 
TSLA and MRNA are behaving reasonably well.
 
TSLA still needs to get through $416 sooner rather than later. The stock continues to build a base, but bulls need a decisive breakout to regain momentum.
 
MRNA is finally beginning to cooperate. We would like to see shares hold today's gains and attract additional buyers throughout the session. If healthcare continues to improve, MRNA should participate.
 
We continue to see several fat-pitch setups developing.
 
SNOW, ARM, and MDB are at the top of our list.
 
Of the three, MDB may offer the most attractive risk-reward profile. The stock appears capable of rallying toward at least $409 based on the current chart structure. We have attached the chart for reference.
 
The bigger story is sector rotation.
 
Enterprise software stocks have lagged the broader technology complex, particularly semiconductors. That is exactly why they are interesting now. As professional money rotates into new leadership groups this summer, software is a logical destination for fresh capital.
 
One final note: we have begun moving out on the curve and into August options. Volatility remains elevated, and additional time provides more flexibility as these setups develop.
 
