Happy Friday,
 
TLDR: ➤ Buy ARM, Sell Entire HOOD, Sell Entire XEL, Sell Half MRNA

 

This feels like an FOMC day.
 
The news flow is intense with the SPCX initial public offering taking center stage, and the S&P 500 is sitting right at 7,415, a key resistance level.
 
If bears lose control of this area, they could find themselves in serious trouble.
 
We knew this rally was coming. The only surprise is the timing. We did not expect it to begin on Thursday ahead of the SPCX offering.
 
Because today is shaping up to be pivotal, traders should expect extreme volatility.
 
There are a lot of SPCX bears waiting to short the stock. These are the same types of investors who shorted TSLA after its debut. They are looking backward instead of forward.
 
Markets reward investors for anticipating where the world is going, not where it has been.
 
Meanwhile, several fat pitches are beginning to set up. We highlighted ARM, SNOW, and CRWD on Thursday. So far, only ARM has started to work.
 
We want exposure, but we do not want to chase strength. Volatility is likely to be extreme today, and patience should create a better entry point.
 
HOOD is acting better, but honestly, we wanted to see an opening print above $95 to feel truly confident about a move toward $102.80. The chart remains constructive, but the stock needs to prove itself. If shares appear likely to close at $94.90 or lower today, we will exit the position.
 
XEL should finally have a tailwind as interest rates begin to moderate. The setup remains reasonable, but we are not interested in giving the trade unlimited room. If shares look likely to close at $79.90 or lower, we will move on.
 
MRNA is finally starting to behave the way we expected. We continue to see a path toward $60 and are adding a profit-taking order on half the position in case volatility creates a sharp upside spike. Support is beginning to form near $48.40.
 
TSLA is OK-ish.
 
The stock still needs to reclaim $415. While traders are linking TSLA to the SPCX offering, we believe that misses the bigger story. The more important catalyst remains quarter-end vehicle deliveries. SPCX may influence sentiment in the near term, but deliveries are what ultimately matter. We are willing to give bulls some room here.
 
The market is finally starting to reward discipline.
 
We spent too much time chasing stories and not enough time focusing on leadership. ARM, SNOW, CRWD, LRCX, KLAC, AMAT, ASML, ALAB, and CRDO remain the names that matter. Those are the fat pitches. Everything else is noise.
 
