Happy Thursday,
 
TLDR: No New Trades

 

The S&P 500 is modestly higher this morning, although well off the levels that seemed likely before the opening bell.
 
The reason is straightforward. Just before the open, President Trump told Fox News that he “may take Kharg Island,” a key Iranian energy export terminal.
 
Every day, stocks are being pushed up and down by comments from President Trump. This is not a new phenomenon. The same dynamic existed throughout his first term.
 
The key for traders is to stay focused on what is actually working and avoid ideas that are easily swept up in the daily news cycle.
 
Admittedly, we have not done a great job of that recently. In fact, we have been terrible. The performance reflects it.
 
We have spent too much time chasing energy stories and special situations in technology that have been difficult to handicap and, at least for us, nearly impossible to trade profitably.
 
To use a baseball analogy, we have been swinging at hellacious sliders and curveballs instead of waiting for our favorite hanging fastball.
 
Coach pulled us aside this week and reminded us of something important. We do not get paid for making contact. We get paid for driving the ball.
 
Lately, we have been chasing pitches outside our sweet spot. We've spent too much time reacting to headlines and not enough time focusing on the stocks that continue to work regardless of the news cycle.
 
The good news is that nothing has changed. The fat pitches are still there. They show up every day.
 
The important thing is recognizing when you've drifted from your strengths and making the adjustment before too many innings get away from you. We've made that adjustment.
 
The game plan is simple: stop chasing bad pitches and start swinging at the ones we know how to hit.
 
The S&P chart is becoming clearer. There is important support at 7,229, right at the rising 50-day moving average. Bulls are probably conceding a test of that level, and they should be aggressive buyers if it happens.
 
At some point soon, there is likely to be a violent short-covering rally back toward 7,414. That process could begin after the SpaceX IPO on Friday or perhaps early next week.
 
When that rally comes, we believe the best stocks to own will be ARM, SNOW, and CRWD. These remain institutional favorites, and they are the types of names that tend to lead when risk appetite returns.
 
In the meantime, the current fat pitches are the same names we discuss every day: LRCX, KLAC, AMAT, ASML, ALAB, and CRDO. These have become the new AI bottleneck stocks.
 
The common theme is that demand remains strong regardless of the headline of the day. You will notice that President Trump has had virtually no impact on their price action.
 
As for our positions, we will sell HOOD tomorrow if shares are not trading at $95 or better.
 
We also need XEL to trade above $80 by tomorrow. If that does not happen, we will close the position and move on.
 
Capital is too valuable to leave tied up in trades that are not working.

 

