Happy Wednesday,
 
TLDR: No New Trades

 

Today is a bit strange because, unless we are missing something, the Consumer Price Index report came in hotter than expected. Yet the yield on the 10-year Treasury note is moving lower.
 
Trading the news is never easy. We understand that. On the surface, this should be a difficult environment for stocks. Instead, the market is holding together remarkably well. That resilience is pulling momentum traders right back into the names that never technically broke down.
 
You know the list: ASML, KLAC, LRCX, and AMAT. Add ALAB and CRDO as well. ALAB touched its 20-day moving average near $309 on Tuesday and now trades at $357. That is an extraordinary move in a very short period of time.
 
The message is clear. Momentum buyers have not gone dormant. They are still buying pullbacks aggressively.
 
The bigger picture is encouraging.
 
The S&P 500 filled its May 5 gap on Wednesday and then staged an impressive reversal into the close. We have included the chart because the move is important.
 
That said, bulls still have work to do. Resistance remains near 7,437, and until that level is cleared decisively, the market is not completely out of the woods.
 
HOOD is delivering the breakout we anticipated. The stock still needs a strong close to confirm the move, but the setup continues to improve. The next meaningful resistance level sits near $102.90.
 
Oddly enough, HOOD now appears to be one of the preferred ways for traders to play the SpaceX IPO theme. We did not see that development coming.
 
TSLA remains sloppy. We continue to like the story and the long-term setup, but the technical picture needs improvement. Bulls must find a way to push shares through $415 sooner rather than later. Until that happens, the stock is likely to remain range-bound.
 
For today, we are content to stay on the sidelines.
 
The market is sending mixed signals, and there is no reason to force new positions when capital is already rotating into existing winners.
 
No new recommendations.

 

