Happy Tuesday,
 
TLDR: No New Trades

 

The chop continues, even as President Trump attempts to reassure markets that the Strait of Hormuz will reopen within days.
 
The S&P 500 opened above the key 7,450 resistance level and rallied as high as 7,483. Sellers quickly emerged, however, and pushed the index back toward unchanged. There is support for the S&P at 7,273, the May 5 high.
 
The President's intervention is important. As always, he is watching the markets closely. He clearly wants higher stock prices and is in a position to help make that happen if progress is made toward ending the conflict with Iran.
 
To be clear, markets will respond to the perception of progress regardless of the facts on the ground.
 
On the other side of the ledger, bears are betting that this week's inflation reports will put additional pressure on interest rates. The Consumer Price Index is due Wednesday, followed by the Producer Price Index on Thursday. Meanwhile, the 10-year Treasury yield remains elevated at 4.54%.
 
Semiconductor equipment stocks continue to surge to record highs. AMAT, ASML, LRCX, and KLAC are all in focus. The trend remains impressive, but this is probably not the tape to chase extended names making fresh highs.
 
Interest-rate-sensitive groups are behaving better. Homebuilders, in particular, are beginning to attract buyers. TOL is breaking out of a constructive consolidation pattern with an initial target of $151 versus the current price near $145.
 
TSLA reclaimed the $414.50 level right on cue, but the early gains are fading somewhat with the broader market. We continue to believe shares are likely to remain anchored near this level until the SpaceX IPO becomes the dominant catalyst.
 
HOOD also staged an early breakout, but those gains are fading alongside the S&P 500. We are willing to give the stock through today's session, but we need to see a close above $87. Without that confirmation, we expect to exit the position tomorrow.
 
The bottom line is that markets remain highly sensitive to headlines coming out of Washington. President Trump wants higher stock prices, but the bond market is demanding tangible progress on inflation. Right now, that means progress on Iran and energy prices. With CPI and PPI reports looming, the window to change the narrative is narrowing.

 

