The S&P 500 is modestly lower. That has been the pattern most mornings over the past two weeks.
 
Oil prices are moving higher and bond yields are climbing as well. That is not an ideal combination for risk assets.
 
One of the stranger developments today is strength in legacy semiconductor stocks as traders bet NVDA's push into personal computing will fail. That thesis misses the mark entirely. NVDA is not coming after the PC market. The prize is the data center. The target is the vast installed base of x86 servers powered by INTC and AMD chips.
 
Ironically, the charts for both INTC and AMD look bullish, not bearish.
 
We did not get filled on TSLA yesterday. We are re-entering the order today.
 
