Happy Monday,
 
TLDR: No New Trades

 

The software rally continues.
 
That is masking real weakness in semiconductors outside of NVDA and ARM. We view that as constructive. Bulls need another group to carry the torch while semis digest recent gains.
 
One concern is the lack of progress on an Iran deal.
 
Reuters reports that Iranian state media is saying Tehran has officially suspended communication with Washington following attacks in Lebanon.
 
West Texas crude oil is up 7.8% to $94.20 per barrel.
 
Higher energy prices are putting pressure on bonds. The 10-year Treasury yield is back above 4.5%. That is not ideal for financial assets.
 
Presidents can fight many battles, but nobody wins a war against the bond market. Consumer discretionary stocks are feeling the pressure.
 
TSLA is one of those names. We like the stock around $420, but we can afford to be patient before initiating new call positions.
 
We remain constructive on CRWV and maintain a target of $137.90. The challenge is that the stock has a habit of retracing after sharp upside gaps. Patience is warranted.
 
We are frustrated that we were unable to establish software call positions. We missed MSFT on Friday despite believing our entry level was attractive and liquidity conditions were favorable. Execution was the issue.
 
TEAM, PLTR, MSFT, and NOW have all reached our initial upside targets. If we owned those positions, we would be inclined to take some profits here.
 
For today, we are content to stand aside. 
 
We need to see whether the weakness in bonds is temporary or the start of something more persistent. Money has a way of finding the weakest link. Right now, the bond market deserves our full attention.

 

