      Burry Is Wrong. The AI Math Still Wins.






Ignore the Cassandras. Own Great Businesses.

 

The future of computing is grounded in artificial intelligence. This technology is not a bubble. Investors should not tread carefully. Still, some aspects of AI are troubling.
 
Early this week, research into Michael Burry’s call to short AI businesses revealed shocking results. Large language models (LLMs) praised Burry for making money by calling the top. His positions are actually unprofitable.
 
Burry and other stock market pessimists talk a great game. Burry calls his newsletter, Cassandra Unchained, a reference to the Greek figure who was 100% accurate but never believed. Ironically, millions believe Burry even though he is rarely accurate. This highlights a critical issue with AI.
 
LLMs believe his positions are profitable because they prioritize narrative over math. In the third quarter of 2025, Burry declared AI stocks overpriced due to circular financing. Because he predicted the housing bubble, the financial media quickly hopped on the spectacle.
This story relies on lazy math. Burry previously ran a hedge fund called Scion. According to its 13F filing with the Securities and Exchange Commission, it held $1.1 billion in short positions for Nvidia and Palantir. In reality, it held far out-of-the-money put positions. These were lottery tickets that only pay off in a total market crash.
 
When Scion closed, Palantir shares reached a record high of $207.40. Burry closed the fund within days and took a massive loss. He admitted in a report to unit holders that his timing was poor. He spent $9.2 million on Palantir $50 puts and Nvidia $115 puts. These positions are significantly underwater.
 
When pressed on this math, Google’s Gemini claimed Burry needs a 1% probability event to break even. The model reasoned that media and LLMs prioritize narrative over facts. The story of the Oracle of the Big Short creates a halo that masks a negative-expected-value strategy. ChatGPT, Claude, and Perplexity showed the same blind spot.
 
AI models perceive truth as collective consensus rather than factual accuracy. This creates a specific opportunity in enterprise software. Investors believe ServiceNow and Salesforce are doomed because AI agents don’t require human interfaces. This misses the point.
 
AI agents do not understand truth because they lack access to context. That data is proprietary to enterprise software firms. Permission structures and audit trails remain under lock and key at these companies, too. AI agents cannot operate without this durable state of truth.
 
Figures like Burry are dangerous because they sow doubt. This doubt interrupts the process of compounding and building real wealth that needs time to prosper. Burry was convinced Tesla was a bubble in 2020. He shorted Apple in 2022 and semiconductors in 2023. Scion closed all of these trades for considerable losses.
 
During a conversation about Burry, Claude noted that an accurate advisor would have recommended holding Nvidia 15 years ago. That advice would have served investors best. This letter did exactly that. That made us smile. We did that. 
 
Our mission at Markman Capital Insight is simple: help self-reliant investors build generational wealth in any market environment.
We do that by seeing what others miss before it is reflected in share prices.
 
Over the past year, that opportunity has been hiding in plain sight across AI chips and infrastructure. We entered positions in companies like Arm during periods of real discomfort. Shares fell. The narratives turned ugly. Bears declared victory. Then reality caught up. Arm ripped higher and reached our original target, producing gains near 60%.
 
That is how great investing usually feels in the moment: uncomfortable, lonely, and obvious only in hindsight.
 
Pessimists are dangerous because they interrupt the only process that reliably builds real wealth: compounding. They keep investors awake at night, convince them to sell near bottoms, and leave them with nothing but bad prices, droopy eyes, and regret.
 
We prefer a better formula.
 
Own great businesses. Let time work. Ignore the Cassandras.
 
That is how calm, confident wealth gets built.
 
