Thesis Document
 
If you are putting new money to work, this is where to start.
 
The May Thesis just dropped. Eleven highest-conviction names, fully refreshed.
 
Earnings season is in the books, and the bulls won decisively. Microsoft, Meta, Palantir, ServiceNow, and Cadence all reported, and the prints came in bigger than the bears expected. Stocks moved up. Fundamentals moved up faster. That is exactly the setup we want.
 
Here is what changed since last month. We raised entry prices on four leaders so the buy points reflect today's reality, not last quarter's. We raised price targets on Arm and Arista because the operating leverage came in larger than we modeled. And we added Tesla as the eleventh name, reframed as the AI and robotics business it has quietly become.
 
Not everything is green. ServiceNow is still underwater. Palantir pulled back. Tesla is brand new with a lot to prove. Those happen to be the most interesting setups for new capital. Bulls do not buy strength alone. They buy weakness in stories that are still working.
 
Here is what matters this month:

Tesla (TSLA) is new to the Buy List. Forget the car business. This is now an AI and robotics story. Cybercab is launching, Optimus is moving onto factory floors, and Megapack is stabilizing the power grid for AI training clusters. AI Day in Palo Alto is the next big catalyst. Buy up to $400. Target: $600.
Nvidia (NVDA) keeps climbing the wall of worry. Shares trade at $196.50, and forward P/E is still only 17.6x. That is astounding for a company growing this fast. GTC Taipei is on June 1, where Jensen Huang is expected to industrialize the Vera Rubin platform. VR200 NVL72 racks are moving into production. HBM4 memory resolves the data bottleneck. Entry raised to $177 from $153.40. Target: $245.
Broadcom (AVGO) is on a tear. Shares ran to $427. AI semiconductor revenue surged 106% last quarter to $8.4 billion. Hock Tan is guiding $22 billion in revenue this quarter and laid out a roadmap to surpass $100 billion in AI chip sales by 2027. Bears called this a bubble. Tan is calling it the new floor. Entry raised to $335 from $308.20. Target: $395.
Arm Holdings (ARM) is the best performer in the portfolio. The Vera CPU is shipping inside Nvidia's next platform. The new AGI CPU pivots Arm from collecting $10 royalties to selling $1,000+ chips direct to hyperscalers. Meta signed on as the first customer. Shares at $208.84. Entry raised to $166 from $122. Target raised to $265 from $225.
Arista Networks (ANET) is taking share at full speed. Cloud and AI titans now account for 48% of revenue. The 800G and 1.6T switching architectures are exactly what hyperscalers need to feed their Blackwell and Rubin clusters. Shares at $170.22. Entry raised to $133.40 from $125.40. Target raised to $190 from $155.
Microsoft (MSFT) is back above our entry at $411.38. Q3 revenue rang in at $82.9 billion, up 18%. Azure accelerated to 40% growth. Satya Nadella raised capex guidance by $35 billion to $190 billion annually. The company is rewiring the world's compute infrastructure on its own dime. Remaining performance obligations doubled to $627 billion. Entry: $405. Target: $620.
Cadence Design Systems (CDNS) keeps quietly compounding. Q1 revenue jumped to $1.47 billion, up 18.6%. The order book stands at $8 billion. April brought the launch of AgentStack for AI-driven chip design. Cadence sits at the heart of every sub-2nm chip being designed today. Entry: $239. Target: $376.
ServiceNow (NOW) is the rare value setup in this portfolio. Shares pulled back to $92.02, well below our $120 entry. Yet Q1 revenue came in at $3.77 billion, up 22%, and beat the high end of management guidance. cRPO grew 21% to $12.64 billion. Sixteen deals over $5 million closed in the quarter, an 80% jump in large deals. The fundamentals are accelerating, and the market is asleep. This is the kind of disconnect long-term investors live for. Entry: $120. Target: $224.
Oracle (ORCL) recovered nicely above our entry to $185.35. The $523 billion backlog remains intact. Larry Ellison is building Solstice, the largest AI supercomputer in America, at Argonne National Laboratory using 100,000 Blackwell GPUs. Multicloud database revenue grew 1,529%. Skeptics keep underestimating Ellison. Entry: $173. Target: $340.
Meta Platforms (META) is just above our entry at $604.96. Q1 revenue rocketed to $56.3 billion, up 33%. That is the fastest growth Meta has posted since 2021. Adjusted EPS came in at $7.31 versus a $6.67 consensus. Ad impressions rose 19% and price per ad rose 12%. The newly formed Meta Superintelligence Labs launched the Muse model family. Zuckerberg is loaded for bear. Entry: $600. Target: $830.
Palantir (PLTR) is in hyper-growth mode. Q1 revenue surged to $1.63 billion, up 85%. US commercial revenue jumped 104%, breaking triple digits for the first time. The company closed 47 contracts worth at least $10 million in the quarter. Adjusted operating margin hit 60%. The Rule of 40 score is 145, an exceptional figure. The multiple is hefty, but the growth is generational. Shares pulled back to $135.91, which gives new buyers a window. Entry: $131.90. Target: $230.

The next leg of this bull market is being built right now. The companies that own the AI and digital transformation buildout are reporting bigger numbers, raising guidance, and grabbing share. Stay long. Stay patient. Stay ready to add on weakness.
 
Read the new thesis. Act accordingly.
 
