      People Will Not Remember Tesla As A Car Company






People Will Not Remember Tesla As A Car Company

 

Action Item: Buy Tesla
Action: BUY
Portfolio Position: Core HoldingPrice Target: Buy Tesla at $400 or less.

 

You may have noticed. The S&P 500 and Nasdaq 100 are back to record highs. This is despite the war with Iran, higher inflation, and near-record consumer gloom about the economy.
 
Understanding stock prices isn’t about geopolitics and macroeconomics. The vast majority of Wall Street economists and analysts have no history of successfully predicting stock prices. For sure, they are bullish now as the indexes make new highs. A month ago, they were universally bearish.
 
Wall Street professionals are making another big mistake. They don’t understand Tesla. That’s a huge opportunity for longer-term investors.
 
Pros think Tesla is an automotive company with a divisive chief executive and dimming financial prospects. This is partly true. Tesla makes cars and trucks that are powered by electricity. Also, Elon Musk certainly has strong opinions that rub many people the wrong way. However, the prospects for Tesla could not be brighter. What the pros are missing is that Tesla isn’t really a car company. The Austin, Texas-based firm makes robots, all kinds of them.
 
With a simple over-the-air software update, all of its consumer vehicles can self-drive. From the budget Model 3 sedan to the beastly Cybertruck, an assortment of cameras and software can navigate city streets and highways, toll roads, parking lots, driveways, and even the drive-thru lanes at fast food restaurants. Tesla’s Full Self-Driving software is an artificial intelligence based system that is light years ahead of the competition. More importantly, FSD is now in the process of rolling out to the rest of the world. Successful pilot programs in Australia and the Netherlands are now underway.
 
The advancement of FSD opens many new business verticals. Tesla launched the Semi prototype in November 2017. The next generation, Class 8 semi-trailer truck promised 500 miles of battery electric range and economics that make diesel semis obsolete. Adding FSD breaks the old economics of short and long-haul trucking into tiny pieces because drivers will be able to drive safer for longer. The regenerative brakes on an electric semi would gather energy from going downhills, not burning diesel. That’s cool, but Tesla has a grander scheme for transportation.
 
Robotaxis break traditional mobility. Most cars and trucks sit idle 94% of the time. They are parked in our driveway, at the mall, or at work, ringing up insurance and maintenance costs. Add fuel expense, and vehicle ownership can be onerous. Robotaxis are driverless traditional and purpose-built Teslas that you request like an Uber. Electrification makes them extremely cheap to operate. Tesla’s lead in FSD and scale manufacturing provides a competitive advantage that should be insurmountable. Robotaxis are the perfect robots, and they are operational today in Austin, San Francisco, Dallas, and Houston.
 
Optimus is a general purpose humanoid robot that is currently in development. At 5 ft 8 inches tall, the 125 pound vision-based robot can carry up to 45 pounds, walk on uneven terrain, sort objects by color, and handle items like eggs without breaking them. The immediate use cases are work in conditions that are too hazardous for humans. This is thinking too small. Elderly care robots from other companies are already operational in Japan. Musk promises that Optimus will be capable of replacing or working alongside human labor. Analysts at Morgan Stanley believe that Optimus is a $5 trillion dollar opportunity.
 
Now add all of the rest of Tesla’s businesses. Vertical integration is Tesla’s super power. It also makes batteries, solar power, and compute at scale. Its Dojo supercomputer trains neural networks for Tesla vehicles, Optimus, and energy systems. This means Tesla has a cost advantage over competitors. It is also uniquely independent from other firms or foundational support.
 
Most of Wall Street is missing these advantages because they are stuck on the cars and Elon Musk. Yet, this is a unique moment in time, like when Jensen Huang pivoted his video game component business to AI. At the time, few people saw what Nvidia was becoming. They were stuck on the video game business and Nvidia’s surging research and development budget. Wall Street saw a business that didn’t make sense. They were universally bearish, and dead wrong.I couldn’t be more bullish on Tesla right now. This weakness is an opportunity to add shares.
 
I am suggesting two things today. First, set up a buy order for Tesla at $400 or below.
 
Second, Tesla has earned its spot as a core holding. This is a big deal. We will continue to accrue shares during drawdowns, and Tesla will be added into our thesis document.
 
For a deeper dive into why Joe has a great research piece on more Tesla angles that makes the opportunity even more exciting.
 
