Happy Tuesday,
 
TLDR: No New Orders

 

The S&P 500 is surging as President Trump says he expects a deal with Iran before the ceasefire expires on Wednesday.
 
More importantly, the president said he will remember companies that do not seek tariff refunds.
 
The quiet part is being said out loud. The tariffs are going away. Completely. Professional money managers are now panic-buying every company negatively impacted by Trump's tariffs.
 
You can see this in strength for retailers like BBY, DDS, M, and TGT. WMT is rising too, although it passed tariffs through to customers. A close above $129.50 would be a massive upside breakout for WMT.
 
RTX reported better-than-expected earnings this morning and raised guidance. Shares are tumbling across all ofaerospace as the president talks about ending the war.
 
We need RTX to end this week above $198.40, its high. If that does not happen by Friday, we will close the position and move on.
 
HOOD and APLD are little changed. The latter is a bit frustrating. APLD had a breakout on Monday. After rising to $34.15 early in the session, shares have quietly drifted lower.
 
We are not surprised that software stocks are rallying ahead of NOW results this week. The carnage in software is the result of a narrative that is entirely untrue. Enterprise software companies are not being disrupted by Anthropic. GM, PEP, JNJ, and other large enterprises have no interest in turning over corporate data to AI black boxes.
 
That is not how culture works inside large companies.
 
No new stocks on our radar. Software names do not meet our criteria, although we like the way they are behaving. With the exception of WMT, the same is true of retailers.

 

