Happy Monday,
 
TLDR: No New Trade

 

The S&P 500 is modestly higher after President Trump said there have been serious talks with Iran to end the war.
 
Traders are not buying it. Energy prices continue to ratchet higher.
 
Worse, we are seeing selling in stock market leaders. This is a sure sign that professional investors are bailing out.
 
Stocks that have been great — CAT, ETN, TER, KLAC, and MTSI — are among the weakest names.
 
Meanwhile, the only pockets of strength are concentrated in the worst relative strength stocks. Enterprise software is showing resilience.
 
The problem is oil. Although President Trump is talking about ending the war, he is also telling the Financial Times that his preference is to "take" Iranian oil.
 
It is difficult to negotiate an end to hostilities when the stated goal is imperialism. The other side has nothing to lose.
 
This is why the war in Ukraine is four years old.
 
We expect a violent short-term rally starting as early as Tuesday. The biggest stock market gains occur within the context of bear cycles.
 
Historically, these rallies have been selling opportunities.
 
We are standing aside until we can find low-risk trades in our patterns.

 

