Happy Wednesday,
 
TLDR: No New Trades

 

Wholesale prices rose 0.7% in February, much hotter than expected. Pundits worry this means the Federal Reserve will not cut interest rates anytime soon.
 
That is not why stocks are weaker this morning, though. The reason is the war in Iran.
 
Oil prices are back above $109 on news that Israel is now bombing Iranian natural gas infrastructure. This is the nightmare scenario for macroeconomic worriers because it takes longer-term energy supply off the market.
 
Despite the energy shock, traders continue to sell defensive sectors like consumer staples. PEP and PG broke support. KO is down 1.6%, far worse than the S&P 500.
 
This is a strange rotation given the macro concerns.
 
Meanwhile, industrials like CAT and DE are holding. Again, strange.
 
There is a decent chance that traders in the oil pits are merely trying to test the March 8 high for Brent crude at $116.66. Brent is currently at $109.50. A reversal lower from that level would be bullish for stocks.
 
We like VST, the big utility company. We have a bullish reversal pattern and a clear target at $178. Unfortunately, option pricing and liquidity are poor.
 
We understand that we are falling behind where we need to be in terms of portfolio growth. It is tough to take trades when the tape is news-dominated and choppy. We will get back on track this week. Be patient.
 
Standing aside one more session.

 

