The S&P 500 is rallying this morning after a Pakistani oil tanker and two LPG vessels successfully crossed the Strait of Hormuz over the weekend.
 
Reuters reports that India is negotiating to move six more vessels.
 
This is the market we are in.
 
Traders are watching the news and oil prices. For now, the news is positive, and prices are falling. West Texas Intermediate crude is down 4.5% to $94.26.
 
More importantly, bulls managed to hold the March 9 lows for the S&P 500. We wrote about that test on Friday at 6,639. The index closed at 6,632.
 
There are no coincidences. This morning's rally is bears covering because they did not get the Monday morning crash.
 
So far, this is an oversold bounce. Banks and brokers are firmer. We mentioned Friday that GS was sitting on its 200-day moving average. GS is up 2.9% this morning.
 
MU blew through our target at $436 and currently trades at $446, up 4.5%. NBIS is up 13.5% after hitting our target at $130 earlier today.
 
We passed on both Friday because we did not want to add calls ahead of the S&P test at 6,639. Sigh.
 
If we had to pick one long right now, it would be ASML. This is a very bullish reversal pattern that should yield new highs above $1,550. Shares are currently at $1,386. The problem, apart from the high price, is option liquidity.
 
On the short side, we do not like the way AMZN and NUE are behaving. These stocks are not rallying with their peers. AMZN could decline to $196. NUE has no support until $155.
 
The bottom line: this is still a news market.
 
It is great that bulls bought the dip again, but the rally could fall apart later today on more bad news from Iran or a careless social media post. There are no easy trades.
 
Standing aside, again.

 

