Happy Thursday,
 
TLDR: No New Trades

 

The S&P 500 is getting a haircut as traders attempt to test the March 9 low at 6,639. The index is currently at 6,690. Rising oil prices are not helping bulls. WTI crude is at $95 per barrel, up 9%.
 
Worse, interest rates are rising. The yield on the 10-year Treasury note is 4.25%. That is bad for mortgage holders and anyone with credit card debt. The news is bad all around. The root cause is the war with Iran.
 
We should not be surprised that everyone is now bearish. This happens into every decline. The oddity is broken stock groups are rallying as bears cover short positions. Enterprise software names like CRM, DSGX, and even DKNG are going straight up.
 
In some cases, the news flow is actually negative. DSGX reported weaker-than-expected results on Wednesday. DKNG is getting new competition from Polymarket in sports betting after the prediction market platform partnered with PLTR. The rallies are short-covering, not conviction.
 
While it might feel good to add puts into this decline, every significant drop since April 2025 has been a buying opportunity. President Trump makes it so by talking up the market late in the session.
 
We are not adding calls, although we like AVGO, INTC, CCJ, and RTX. We missed RBLX puts on Wednesday. Shares are trading only modestly lower.
 
We understand that some of you were able to add AVGO calls on Wednesday. Although we did not officially enter the position — our orders were not filled — we remain extremely bullish on AVGO and will track it. We are likely to add calls, perhaps as early as Friday or early next week. We merely want to wait for the tape to settle.
 
Stand aside for now.

 

