Happy Monday,
 
No New Orders

 

The S&P 500 is mixed after Wall Street analysts took a closer look at capital expenditure plans from META, GOOGL, MSFT, and AMZN.
 
The conclusion: new data centers are going to be built after all.
 
This was never really in doubt. CEOs at these companies have been saying—plainly and repeatedly—that capex would rise, not fall. Anyone who bothered to listen already knew this. Now the usual beneficiaries are screaming higher.
 
HUT is up another 5% today after rallying nearly 20% on Friday. CRWV is up 8%. Our upside targets remain $62.70 and $106, respectively.
 
A close above $194 for NVDA would likely trigger short covering and a run to new highs. That puts the stock back in the $210 zone quickly.
CCJ is a nuclear name that fits this environment well. Shares could rally to $130. The stock is currently $118.60.
 
DG remains our favorite non-tech idea, but the upside is limited. We only see shares trading to about $155, so we are passing for now.
C reached our target at $124. If you are still holding calls, be careful up here. This is where traders tend to get greedy.
 
AA remains under close watch. We need shares to stay below $59.50 on a closing basis to keep the bearish thesis intact.
 
OKLO is up 5.5% and moving toward the $81–$82 zone. That move could happen today.
 
RXRX is flat. There is minor resistance at $4.25.
 
No new recommendations today.

 

