Happy Wednesday,
 
TLDR: ➤ Sell half of C March (Day Order Only)

 

The S&P 500 is testing its 50-day moving average at 6,877. This level has to hold for bulls. A failure today likely means a swift test of 6,800.
 
What’s unusual is the strength in early-cycle stock groups even as the 10-year Treasury yield pushes higher. Rising rates are not supposed to be friendly for these stocks, yet homebuilders, truckers, regional banks, and industrials are all catching strong bids. This tells us money is rotating, not fleeing.
 
Early-cycle leaders and targets:
 
Homebuilders / Housing-related

Toll Brothers (TOL): $155
Sherwin-Williams (SHW): $380
Home Depot (HD): $405.70
Stanley Black & Decker (SWK): $87.10

Heavy vehicles / Truckers

J.B. Hunt (JBHT): $235

Regional Banks

PNC Financial (PNC): $245.30
Zions Bancorp (ZION): $66.50

Industrials

Oshkosh (OSK): $177.20
General Motors (GM): $94.50
Eaton (ETN): $392

On the flip side, we continue to believe metals are a short. Silver just bounced back to its 50-day moving average and stalled. There is no shortage of precious or base metals. LME inventories continue to signal gluts, not scarcity.
 
We’re also seeing clear loss of momentum in several former favorites. GSAT, HOOD, LMND, and W all fit this category. We are going to focus on GSAT. Shares look headed to at least $51.20.
 
Citi opened stronger but is now hovering near $120. We still see a move to $124, but risk management matters. We are scaling out rather than pressing.
 
AA closed strong Tuesday and pushed higher again today, but shares are now reversing sharply. A close below $59.60 would decisively knock out the bulls.
 
OKLO is testing the December low near $70. Shares should bounce here, but the broader trend remains bearish until bulls can reclaim $88.
 
Trade Management
 
➤ Cancel order to sell C March 20th $115 calls at $11.70 or greater (GTC)
 
➤ Sell half of C March 20th $115 calls at $8.20 or greater (day order only)

 

