Happy Thursday,
TLDR: Entered in AA & OLKO Yesterday

 

The S&P is slicing through its 20-day moving average at 6,915. The next meaningful support is 6,850, the rising 50-day moving average.
We are not surprised bears chose to make their stand at 7,000. That level mattered. What is surprising is the makeup of the selling.
Enterprise software stocks are getting hit after MSFT posted another solid quarter. Some of this is fear around AI disruption. That’s fine.
Traders don’t argue with narratives—they follow price. When support fails, stocks get sold.
We are also watching commodities closely. Gold is printing a massive one-day bearish reversal. It’s still early (11:00 ET), but the action is notable. GLD traded to a new high at $509.70 this morning, up more than 3%, and is now at $478, down nearly 5% on enormous volume.
That is not noise.
We added OKLO calls and AA puts on Wednesday.
We are short AA because metals are extremely extended and Chinese industrial demand is rolling over fast. AA opened $3 higher and is now lower on the day. That’s encouraging. A close below $57.50 should kick off a move toward our $51 target.
OKLO is down roughly $10 today, which is surprising given it closed at its high yesterday. Our calls were briefly up nearly 30% before the reversal. Support sits near $81. Bulls need to close this stock near $90 to keep the setup intact. That’s still doable.
It feels too late to chase puts in weak groups like enterprise software. More importantly, history has been very clear over the past year: bullish breakouts fail, but pullbacks to major support tend to become launchpads for the next rally.
We expect buyers to re-emerge as the S&P approaches 6,850.
Stand aside for now.

 

