      AI Fear Is Creating a Software Buying Window






AI Fear Is Creating a Software Buying Window

 

You may have noticed that recently both AI infrastructure and enterprise software stocks are getting hammered. This is leading to a rare opportunity for longer-term investors.
Traders are now betting that traditional software businesses are being disrupted and that AI as a technology will not live up to its advanced billing as the "next big thing." Both of these bets cannot possibly be true simultaneously. Something has to give.
Let’s get ready to buy enterprise software.  Software is the third layer of the AI pyramid (more on this in future letters). 
It is important to understand what is happening. Professional money managers believe shares of the biggest tech companies—the so-called "Magnificent Seven"—are now dramatically overweighted in the S&P 500. To be clear, this is not because the companies are terrible businesses; in fact, the opposite is true. Unfortunately for the pros, this heavy concentration makes it nearly impossible for them to keep pace with their benchmark. 
It’s a really bad look with clients.
According to data from S&P Global and Morningstar, between 59% and 65% of actively managed U.S. large-capitalization funds underperformed the S&P during 2025. That’s the worst in two decades. The damage was even worse when small-capitalization and international equities were included. Those categories underperformed at the shocking rates of 78% and 85%, respectively.
So, pros are underweight stocks like Nvidia, Alphabet, Apple, Microsoft, Amazon.com, Meta Platforms, and Tesla, even though they believe these companies hold strong competitive advantages. The hope is that "Mag-7" shares will perform poorly relative to their small-cap and international cohorts. That’s the theory.
While Mag-7 shares are facing a lack of sponsorship from pros, enterprise software stocks are being ravaged by smaller investors who believe that AI is about to disrupt everything.
In fairness, we believe AI is a secular game-changer that is bigger than the internet, cloud, and mobile combined. All of the world’s computers are in the process of changing from traditional store-and-retrieve architectures to generative ones. The days of grabbing data stored on a hard drive somewhere are slowly coming to an end. The keyword here is slowly.
Alex Rampell, Jennifer Li, and David Haber manage AI application investment at Andreessen Horowitz, the storied venture capital fund. The trio recently hosted a podcast about AI adoption at the enterprise level. Their bottom line is that the most successful AI startups are not attacking traditional enterprise software; instead, they are building completely new "greenfield" business categories, such as labor.
Rampell says that a startup called Eve completely automated workflows for employment and personal injury lawyers. Eve’s AI agents automatically process evidence, medical documents, demand letters, complaints, and case valuations. This is the behind-the-scenes legal work that is expensive because it is labor-intensive. Eve leads to more profitable caseloads.
More importantly, the Andreessen Horowitz team found that strong competitive business moats are defensible and extremely difficult to penetrate. Rampell says the best companies have "hostages, not customers." Think about the cost for an enterprise to switch from the Microsoft Office productivity suite, for you to dump your iPhone, or to stop shopping at Amazon.com.
The Mag-7 continue to grow because they have competitive advantages. The largest enterprise software companies have cost-of-switching advantages, too. Moreover, their customers are extremely conservative. They adopt new technology slowly. There is that word again.
The good news for longer-term investors is that we are currently in the middle of the fourth-quarter earnings season. This means that enterprise software companies and AI infrastructure businesses will soon begin reporting Q4 and 2025 financial results. Given that there have been no significant earnings warnings to date, we expect those numbers to be consistent with Q3. That should be a win for bullish investors.
We are looking for safe new entry levels for Oracle, Microsoft, and CrowdStrike (CRWD), a company that makes cybersecurity software. We will have more on CrowdStrike and others in the Thursday Letter.
