The S&P opened higher on strength in financials and some bargain hunting in big tech.
Unfortunately, traders are once again selling into the early strength.
This is starting to feel uncomfortably familiar.
Like most traders, we prefer to buy breakouts and the first pullback to the breakout level. That setup gives us a clean risk framework.
If the trade fails, we usually get a second chance to exit near breakeven.
That matters.
We are far less comfortable buying pullbacks to major support.
Ironically, many of the best trades in the second half of 2025—and now early 2026—have come from exactly those setups.
We often pass anyway because when major support fails, the damage for short-term traders is usually unrecoverable.
We have clear goals for this account.
We now have critical mass and the ability to generate roughly $3,000 to $4,000 per month on an account that started at $10,000.
That is real money.
That is the power of compounding.
Because of that, our priority is not trade frequency.
It’s trade quality.
We are perfectly willing to do less if it means deploying capital only when the odds are overwhelmingly in our favor.
The AI trade remains interesting.
One of the biggest tells is the relative strength in energy and infrastructure companies tied directly to data center buildouts.
VRT stands out.
Shares look destined for at least $200, but we only want exposure on weakness.
IONQ is frustratingly close to a breakout.
The problem today is option expiration.
Stocks have a bad habit of getting pinned near major strikes, and IONQ has heavy open interest at $50 and $55.
That changes the math.
Our position must be sold today.
We were stopped out of WMT at $7.45.
